1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,950
doeff is a Python library for algebraic effects.

2
00:00:06,150 --> 00:00:12,650
This video shows what algebraic effects are, and then how you write them with doeff.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:19,600
What are algebraic effects for? Four things a program does besides computing.

4
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:24,700
Side effects: printing, saving a file, sending a message.

5
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,950
Dependencies: a value the program needs from outside.

6
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:38,400
Async and scheduling: waiting for a second, a turn or a click, retrying, running many tasks at once.

7
00:00:39,550 --> 00:00:45,800
And swapping: the same program runs in tests, in replay, in production.

8
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:53,600
One mechanism does all four. Asking for a value is the simplest use, so we start there.

9
00:00:55,450 --> 00:01:03,300
Our example is tiny: a program that buys tea. It needs two things from outside: a price, and a yes.

10
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:10,250
With algebraic effects, the program only says what it needs. That request is an effect.

11
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:16,050
How to get it is decided outside the program, by a handler.

12
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:21,250
The handler answers, and the program goes on from where it asked.

13
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:30,150
You know half of this already: an exception. It is raised inside, and caught by a handler outside.

14
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:35,600
But after the catch, the program never goes back to where it raised.

15
00:01:36,750 --> 00:01:41,950
An effect handler sends an answer back, and the program picks up where it left off.

16
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:49,650
So the program says what it needs, and the handlers decide how.

17
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:59,000
Now the same idea in doeff. The tea program is a Python function, order, marked with @do.

18
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:06,200
Where it needs something, it yields an effect. First Price: what does the item cost?

19
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:13,700
Then Confirm: may I pay that much? Then it returns. It never says who answers.

20
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:22,400
Handlers sit outside the function. prices answers Price with 3, and order goes on.

21
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:29,300
With Confirm, the handler also gets k: the rest of order, paused at the yield.

22
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:35,800
It resumes k with True, and order returns bought, 3.

23
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:43,750
It could also keep k for later, or never resume it. Either way, k is resumed at most once.

24
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:50,200
Keeping k for later is the third use: scheduling. Take a card game.

25
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:56,017
A Poison card hurts the enemy at the end of each turn, for three turns.

26
00:02:56,017 --> 00:03:02,450
The fight spawns it as a task of its own: a plain loop that waits for the turn to end, then hurts.

27
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:09,583
TurnEnds is an effect. Its handler, the turn clock, makes a promise and waits on it.

28
00:03:09,583 --> 00:03:15,300
So doeff's scheduler keeps k, the rest of poison, until the fight ends the turn.

29
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:18,150
Then k is resumed, and the poison hurts.

30
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:25,600
A damage number that rises on screen is the same loop on another clock: it waits a tenth of a second.

31
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:32,000
Two handlers, stacked: two kinds of time, with no locks and no events.

32
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:35,833
The fourth use is swapping.

33
00:03:35,833 --> 00:03:42,500
Handlers stack around the function: swap the stack, and the same function runs another way.

34
00:03:43,650 --> 00:03:50,300
Tests, replay, a bot, many at once, production. order does not change a line.

35
00:03:52,650 --> 00:03:58,100
The article needs a bigger program, so it uses the whole card fight: battle.

36
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:02,033
Each turn you draw a hand and play cards.

37
00:04:02,033 --> 00:04:07,450
At its end a Poison hurts, and the enemy hits back, until one side is out of HP.

38
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,933
It is a good example: it needs five things from outside.

39
00:04:12,933 --> 00:04:17,950
Dice, a choice of card, a screen, a count, and the end of a turn.

40
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:25,450
And they can be answered in many ways: by a script, a bot, a tape, or a person.

41
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,700
The article runs battle under six stacks, and does not change a line of it.

42
00:04:33,550 --> 00:04:41,000
To start, pip install doeff. Then you need three things: @do, yield, and a handler.
